  "analysis": "\n\n# Dataset Analysis for Rating Prediction\n\n## Task Type & Target\n- **Task**: Regression (predicting movie ratings)\n- **Target**: `rating` (float64, range 0.5–5.0, 10 discrete values, mean=3.502)\n- **Note**: Although technically regression, the target has only 10 discrete levels—could alternatively be treated as ordinal classification. This affects metric choice (RMSE/MAE vs. weighted kappa).\n\n## Target Distribution\n- Not a classification task, but note the target is **discrete ordinal** (0.5 increments)\n- No missing values in target\n- Mean of 3.5 suggests roughly symmetric distribution around mid-range, but verify skew before modeling\n\n## Column Characteristics\n\n| Column | Type | Cardinality | Notes |\n|--------|------|-------------|-------|\n| `userId` | Categorical (int64 ID) | 600 | Low-cardinality user identifier |\n| `movieId` | Categorical (int64 ID) | 3,979 | Medium-cardinality item identifier |\n| `rating` | Target (float64) | 10 | Discrete ordinal scale |\n\n## Candidate Encoders & Preprocessing\n\n| Column | Recommended Encoders (search options) |\n|--------|---------------------------------------|\n| `userId` | TargetEncoder, EmbeddingEncoder, or simple LabelEncoding (low cardinality) |\n| `movieId` | TargetEncoder, MinHashEncoder, or EmbeddingEncoder (medium cardinality) |\n| `userId` + `movieId` interaction | Consider concatenating as a pair feature for collaborative filtering signal |\n\n**Cleaning Steps**:\n- No missing values to impute\n- Convert `userId` and `movieId` to string type before encoding (treat as categoricals, not numerics)\n- No scaling needed for ID columns post-encoding\n\n## Feature Engineering Options\n\n1. **User-level aggregations**: Mean rating per user, rating count per user, std dev of user ratings\n2. **Movie-level aggregations**: Mean rating per movie, rating count per movie (from main table)\n3. **Interaction features**: User × movie cross features if using factorization models\n\n## Specific Column Treatments\n\n- **`userId`**: Treat as categorical despite int64 dtype. Consider user embedding or target encoding with regularization to avoid leakage.\n- **`movieId`**: Same as userId. High-cardinality relative to row count (3979 movies / 11999 rows ≈ 3 ratings/movie average)—regularization critical.\n\n## Auxiliary Table Opportunities ('movies')\n\n**Join Key**: `movieId` ↔ `movieId` (100% overlap)\n\n| Aux Column | Type | Treatment Options |\n|------------|------|-------------------|\n| `title` | Free text (3979 unique) | GapEncoder, MinHashEncoder, or extract year from title (e.g., \"Toy Story (1995)\") |\n| `genres` | Multi-label categorical (654 unique) | MultiLabelBinarizer, or split by delimiter then OneHot/TargetEncode top-N genres |\n\n**Predictive Aggregations from 'movies'**:\n- Genre-based mean ratings (aggregate historical ratings by genre)\n- Title year extraction → decade features (older vs. newer movies may rate differently)\n- Genre count (number of genres per movie)\n\n## Candidate Model Families\n\n1. **Gradient Boosting** (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost)\n   - Handles mixed encodings well\n   - CatBoost has native categorical support\n  \n2. **Factorization Machines / Neural Collaborative Filtering**\n   - Designed for user-item interaction tasks\n   - Embedding-based approaches for userId/movieId\n  \n3. **Linear Models with Regularization** (Ridge, ElasticNet)\n   - Baseline with target-encoded features\n   - Fast to train, good for comparison\n\n## Pipeline Search Space Summary\n\n| Stage | Options to Search |\n|-------|-------------------|\n| ID Encoding | TargetEncoder (with cv folds), EmbeddingEncoder, MinHashEncoder |\n| Aux Text (`title`) | GapEncoder vs. MinHashEncoder vs. extract year only |\n| Aux Genres (`genres`) | MultiLabelBinarizer + OneHot vs. TargetEncoder on genre string |\n| Aggregations | User stats, movie stats, genre stats (join back to main) |\n| Models | CatBoostRegressor, LightGBMRegressor, XGBRegressor, FactorizationMachine |\n| Validation | GroupKFold by userId (prevent user leakage across folds) |\n\n## Key Risks & Considerations\n\n- **Data Leakage**: Target encoding must use out-of-fold strategies; user/movie aggregates must be computed per-fold\n- **Sparsity**: ~3 ratings per movie average—regularization essential for movie-level features\n- **Cold Start**: New users/movies won't have embeddings; consider fallback features (genre averages, global mean)\n- **Validation Strategy**: Use GroupKFold on `userId` to ensure no user appears in both train and test folds",
